1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,584
Stand still in the rain,
and it falls straight down.

2
00:00:03,584 --> 00:00:05,862
Run, and it seems to hit your face.

3
00:00:06,262 --> 00:00:09,356
In a moving bus, raindrops
slant across the window.

4
00:00:09,356 --> 00:00:11,487
Did the rain change, or did you?

5
00:00:11,937 --> 00:00:16,191
Aarav cycles to college in the rain,
holding his umbrella straight up.

6
00:00:16,191 --> 00:00:18,397
His shirt still gets wet in front.

7
00:00:18,747 --> 00:00:20,373
The rain has not changed.

8
00:00:20,373 --> 00:00:23,959
So which way should he tilt
his umbrella, and by how much?

9
00:00:24,459 --> 00:00:27,646
By the end of this video, you
will find the exact angle.

10
00:00:28,296 --> 00:00:31,572
So what is relative
velocity in two dimensions?

11
00:00:31,922 --> 00:00:36,335
It is the velocity of one object as
seen by another, when both move in

12
00:00:36,335 --> 00:00:37,235
a plane.

13
00:00:37,482 --> 00:00:40,109
In one dimension, we subtracted numbers.

14
00:00:40,109 --> 00:00:42,348
In a plane, we subtract vectors.

15
00:00:42,748 --> 00:00:44,641
The rule stays the same.

16
00:00:44,641 --> 00:00:46,955
v RA equals v R minus v A.

17
00:00:46,955 --> 00:00:49,831
The rain's velocity, minus the watcher's.

18
00:00:50,481 --> 00:00:52,781
Let's test it in our motion notebook.

19
00:00:52,781 --> 00:00:55,892
Rain falls straight down
at five metres per second.

20
00:00:56,242 --> 00:00:59,031
From the ground, the
drops fall straight down.

21
00:00:59,031 --> 00:01:00,261
Aarav stands still.

22
00:01:00,661 --> 00:01:02,468
Now Aarav starts to ride.

23
00:01:02,468 --> 00:01:05,359
His speed climbs to
five metres per second.

24
00:01:05,759 --> 00:01:06,973
Switch the view.

25
00:01:06,973 --> 00:01:08,543
Ride along with Aarav.

26
00:01:08,943 --> 00:01:11,335
From his seat, the drops slant toward him.

27
00:01:11,335 --> 00:01:13,228
They seem to come from the front.

28
00:01:13,728 --> 00:01:17,217
On the graph, the faster he
rides, the bigger the slant.

29
00:01:17,217 --> 00:01:19,643
At five, it reaches forty five degrees.

30
00:01:20,143 --> 00:01:21,643
Slow motion replay.

31
00:01:21,993 --> 00:01:24,567
Here is the rain's arrow, straight down.

32
00:01:24,567 --> 00:01:26,602
Here is Aarav's arrow, forward.

33
00:01:27,002 --> 00:01:29,771
Flip Aarav's arrow, and
add it to the rain's.

34
00:01:29,771 --> 00:01:33,104
The result is the rain he
sees, slanting back at him.

35
00:01:33,754 --> 00:01:35,416
Now let's find the angle.

36
00:01:35,416 --> 00:01:36,840
One step at a time.

37
00:01:37,190 --> 00:01:38,141
Step one.

38
00:01:38,141 --> 00:01:39,871
Write both in parts.

39
00:01:39,871 --> 00:01:43,283
Rain is zero along x, minus five along y.

40
00:01:43,283 --> 00:01:45,558
Aarav is plus five along x.

41
00:01:46,308 --> 00:01:47,246
Step two.

42
00:01:47,246 --> 00:01:49,064
Subtract part by part.

43
00:01:49,064 --> 00:01:52,209
v RA is minus five i, minus five j.

44
00:01:52,209 --> 00:01:55,262
Five back toward Aarav, and five down.

45
00:01:56,012 --> 00:01:56,927
Step three.

46
00:01:56,927 --> 00:02:00,566
Its size is the root of five
squared plus five squared.

47
00:02:00,566 --> 00:02:04,553
Five root two, about seven point
oh seven metres per second.

48
00:02:05,303 --> 00:02:06,227
Step four.

49
00:02:06,227 --> 00:02:08,297
The angle from the vertical.

50
00:02:08,297 --> 00:02:11,843
Tan theta equals Aarav's
speed over the rain's speed.

51
00:02:11,843 --> 00:02:15,781
Five over five is one, so
theta is forty five degrees.

52
00:02:16,531 --> 00:02:17,546
Remember this.

53
00:02:17,546 --> 00:02:21,842
Hold the umbrella against the rain
you see, not the rain that falls.

54
00:02:22,542 --> 00:02:23,804
Side by side.

55
00:02:24,154 --> 00:02:25,529
Aarav stands still.

56
00:02:25,529 --> 00:02:27,951
The rain he sees is just the rain.

57
00:02:27,951 --> 00:02:29,425
Umbrella straight up.

58
00:02:29,825 --> 00:02:31,355
Aarav rides at five.

59
00:02:31,355 --> 00:02:34,262
The rain he sees tilts forty five degrees.

60
00:02:34,262 --> 00:02:37,897
So he tilts the umbrella
forward, forty five degrees.

61
00:02:38,497 --> 00:02:39,580
Now you try.

62
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:41,356
Aarav rides even faster.

63
00:02:41,356 --> 00:02:44,435
Should he tilt the umbrella more, or less?

64
00:02:44,435 --> 00:02:45,780
Pause and decide.

65
00:02:48,980 --> 00:02:49,880
More!

66
00:02:49,521 --> 00:02:52,406
Tan theta is his speed
over the rain's speed.

67
00:02:52,406 --> 00:02:54,790
A bigger speed gives a bigger angle.

68
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:58,359
The same idea steers
a boat across a river.

69
00:02:58,359 --> 00:02:59,630
Follow the links.

70
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:00,880
One.

71
00:03:00,468 --> 00:03:02,627
The boat moves through the water.

72
00:03:02,627 --> 00:03:05,380
That is its velocity
relative to the water.

73
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:06,680
Two.

74
00:03:06,274 --> 00:03:10,199
The water itself flows downstream,
and carries the boat along.

75
00:03:10,599 --> 00:03:11,499
Three.

76
00:03:11,162 --> 00:03:15,021
Seen from the bank, the boat's
velocity is the sum of the two.

77
00:03:15,421 --> 00:03:16,321
Four.

78
00:03:15,964 --> 00:03:19,142
So a boat pointed straight
across lands downstream.

79
00:03:19,742 --> 00:03:20,642
One more.

80
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:22,857
The boat must land exactly opposite.

81
00:03:22,857 --> 00:03:26,262
Should it point straight
across, or slightly upstream?

82
00:03:26,262 --> 00:03:27,448
Pause and think.

83
00:03:30,648 --> 00:03:31,867
Slightly upstream!

84
00:03:31,867 --> 00:03:36,657
Part of its velocity then cancels the
river, and the rest carries it across.

85
00:03:37,307 --> 00:03:39,043
Now, one worked example.

86
00:03:39,393 --> 00:03:43,896
A river is one hundred metres wide,
and flows at two metres per second.

87
00:03:43,896 --> 00:03:48,532
A boat points straight across, at five
metres per second through the water.

88
00:03:49,032 --> 00:03:53,095
Across the river, only the boat's
five metres per second helps.

89
00:03:53,095 --> 00:03:55,181
Time is one hundred over five.

90
00:03:55,181 --> 00:03:56,288
Twenty seconds.

91
00:03:57,038 --> 00:04:01,122
In those twenty seconds, the river
carries it downstream at two metres

92
00:04:01,122 --> 00:04:02,022
per second.

93
00:04:01,982 --> 00:04:03,421
That is forty metres.

94
00:04:04,171 --> 00:04:08,813
Seen from the bank, its speed is the
root of five squared plus two squared.

95
00:04:08,813 --> 00:04:11,627
About five point three
nine metres per second.

96
00:04:12,277 --> 00:04:13,742
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:14,092 --> 00:04:18,715
Many students add the two velocities
to find the rain Aarav sees.

98
00:04:18,715 --> 00:04:20,172
Rain plus cyclist.

99
00:04:20,522 --> 00:04:23,133
But relative velocity is a subtraction.

100
00:04:23,133 --> 00:04:24,541
Rain minus cyclist.

101
00:04:24,541 --> 00:04:28,059
Adding would make the rain
seem to come from behind.

102
00:04:28,709 --> 00:04:30,178
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:30,178 --> 00:04:34,563
Rain and umbrella problems, river
crossings, and two moving objects.

104
00:04:35,163 --> 00:04:36,373
Exam level now.

105
00:04:36,373 --> 00:04:41,189
A cyclist rides east at ten metres
per second, and the rain seems to fall

106
00:04:41,189 --> 00:04:42,286
straight down.

107
00:04:42,286 --> 00:04:46,826
At twenty, it seems to fall at
forty five degrees to the vertical.

108
00:04:46,826 --> 00:04:48,978
Find the rain's actual velocity.

109
00:04:48,978 --> 00:04:50,238
Pause and solve.

110
00:04:55,638 --> 00:04:57,018
The answer is B.

111
00:04:57,518 --> 00:04:59,682
Let the rain be a i minus b j.

112
00:04:59,682 --> 00:05:03,433
At ten, the rain looks vertical,
so a minus ten is zero.

113
00:05:03,433 --> 00:05:04,333
a is ten.

114
00:05:04,765 --> 00:05:07,347
At twenty, the x part is minus ten.

115
00:05:07,347 --> 00:05:11,270
At forty five degrees, the
y part matches, so b is ten.

116
00:05:11,770 --> 00:05:14,157
So the rain is ten i minus ten j.

117
00:05:14,157 --> 00:05:18,687
Ten root two metres per second, at
forty five degrees to the vertical.

118
00:05:19,287 --> 00:05:20,748
Here is your recap.

119
00:05:20,748 --> 00:05:22,249
Screenshot this card.

120
00:05:25,449 --> 00:05:28,448
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

121
00:05:28,848 --> 00:05:29,748
One.

122
00:05:29,291 --> 00:05:31,763
Why does rain seem to slant when you run?

123
00:05:32,663 --> 00:05:33,563
Two.

124
00:05:33,135 --> 00:05:36,861
Rain falls at eight metres per
second, and you ride at six.

125
00:05:36,861 --> 00:05:39,577
At what angle should
you tilt your umbrella?

126
00:05:40,477 --> 00:05:41,377
Three.

127
00:05:41,072 --> 00:05:44,576
Why does a boat pointed
straight across land downstream?

128
00:05:45,676 --> 00:05:50,238
Next time: flick a card out from under
a coin, and the coin drops straight

129
00:05:50,238 --> 00:05:51,138
down.

130
00:05:50,765 --> 00:05:52,026
Why does it stay?

131
00:05:52,026 --> 00:05:54,829
We start chapter four
with force and inertia.
